# .github/workflows/external-intake.yml
# Third entry channel: a division's own repository requests a perimeter change.
#
# Same principle as ServiceNow intake: the payload is DATA, not authorization. Here the proof is different —
# instead of calling a ticketing system back, we check the request against perimeter/contributors.yaml,
# which lives HERE and can only be changed with networking + security approval. A repository cannot widen
# its own list of allowed projects, because that list is not in its repository.
#
# WHY workflow_dispatch AND NOT repository_dispatch. The trigger is chosen by the PERMISSION it forces on
# the other side, not by taste. `repository_dispatch` can only be sent with Contents WRITE — the right to
# write code in THIS repository. Compose that with a default branch without branch protection (a paid
# feature for private repositories on several GitHub plans) and with apply running automatically from that
# branch, and a division's credential stops being "the right to ask" and becomes a path to mutating the
# organization's boundary with every gate bypassed: schema, OPA, budget and pre-flight all hang off
# `pull_request`, and a push straight to the default branch runs none of them. `workflow_dispatch` needs
# `actions: write`, which starts workflows and writes no code — measured, and disjoint from `contents` in
# both directions.
#
# ONE channel, not two. `repository_dispatch: vpc-sc-external` was REMOVED rather than kept alongside for
# a transition period: while both are live the wider permission is still the one the sender must hold, so
# the narrowing buys nothing and the material would document a requirement that is not the real one.
# (`intake.yml` — the ServiceNow channel — still listens on `repository_dispatch`. Its sender is the
# ticketing integration, a different credential with a different owner; see docs/5-servicenow-intake.md.)
#
# WHAT `actions: write` STILL ALLOWS, said out loud rather than left flattering: re-running and cancelling
# workflows in this repository, and DELETING run logs. Narrower than code write, not zero. The audit trail
# that matters therefore lives in git — the pull request and its commit — not only in run history.

name: external-intake

on:
  # Inputs are flat strings (max 10, 65535 characters each), so the nested declaration travels as ONE
  # input holding JSON. `project_id` is repeated as its own input because `concurrency` is evaluated
  # before any step runs and cannot parse JSON; the first step refuses the request when the two disagree.
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      member:
        description: The member declaration as a JSON object (the division's request.yaml plus change_ref).
        required: true
      project_id:
        description: Project id from that declaration, repeated flat for the concurrency group.
        required: true
      source_repository:
        description: ORG/REPO of the requesting repository.
        required: true
      source_sha:
        description: Commit in the requesting repository the request was built from.
        required: true
      change_ref:
        description: "Reference to the approved change, `pr:ORG/REPO#NUMBER`."
        required: true

permissions:
  contents: write # the bot commits the member file on a branch of THIS repository
  pull-requests: write

concurrency:
  group: external-intake-${{ github.event.inputs.project_id }}
  cancel-in-progress: false

jobs:
  intake:
    name: validate an external request and open a PR
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      # The dispatch endpoint takes a `ref` and runs the workflow FILE as it exists there. The sender picks
      # that ref. It cannot create one — `actions: write` writes no code — but branches left behind by open
      # pull requests do exist, and the version of this file on such a branch is not the version that was
      # reviewed. Refusing anything but the default branch costs three lines and closes that gap from this
      # side; the action closes it from the other by reading the default branch from the API.
      - name: only the default branch handles external requests
        env:
          DEFAULT_BRANCH: ${{ github.event.repository.default_branch }}
        run: |
          [ "$GITHUB_REF_NAME" = "$DEFAULT_BRANCH" ] || {
            echo "::error::zgloszenie zewnetrzne uruchomione na '$GITHUB_REF_NAME', a nie na '$DEFAULT_BRANCH'"
            exit 1
          }

      - uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262 # v4.4.0

      - uses: actions/setup-python@a26af69be951a213d495a4c3e4e4022e16d87065 # v5.6.0
        with:
          python-version: "3.12"

      - name: install tooling
        run: |
          pip install --quiet pyyaml check-jsonschema
          curl -sSfL -o /tmp/conftest.tar.gz \
            https://github.com/open-policy-agent/conftest/releases/download/v0.56.0/conftest_0.56.0_Linux_x86_64.tar.gz
          tar -xzf /tmp/conftest.tar.gz -C /tmp conftest
          sudo mv /tmp/conftest /usr/local/bin/

      # WHO SENT THIS, recorded before anything is decided on it. `github.triggering_actor` is written by
      # GitHub, not by the sender, so unlike every field of the payload it cannot be spoofed — but it names
      # the APP INSTALLATION, not the repository, and one App installed on thirty division repositories
      # produces the same actor for all thirty. So it is evidence of WHICH CREDENTIAL was used, not of which
      # repository used it. That second question is answered by `contributors.yaml` below, and by nothing
      # else. Writing this down is the point: an audit trail described as stronger than it is, is worse than
      # a weak one honestly labelled.
      - name: record who dispatched this
        env:
          ACTOR: ${{ github.triggering_actor }}
          SOURCE_REPO: ${{ inputs.source_repository }}
          SOURCE_SHA: ${{ inputs.source_sha }}
          CHANGE_REF: ${{ inputs.change_ref }}
        run: |
          {
            echo "| ślad | wartość |"
            echo "|---|---|"
            echo "| \`triggering_actor\` (od GitHuba, niepodrabialny) | \`$ACTOR\` |"
            echo "| \`source_repository\` (deklarowane w payloadzie) | \`$SOURCE_REPO\` |"
            echo "| \`source_sha\` (deklarowane w payloadzie) | \`$SOURCE_SHA\` |"
            echo "| \`change_ref\` (ustawiane przez akcję contrib) | \`$CHANGE_REF\` |"
            echo
            echo "Aktor identyfikuje INSTALACJĘ aplikacji, nie repozytorium — o to, czy TO repozytorium może"
            echo "prosić o TEN projekt, pyta \`perimeter/contributors.yaml\` niżej."
          } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"

      # The payload is parsed HERE, from an environment variable, and never interpolated into a shell or a
      # Python literal: `${{ inputs.member }}` pasted into code would let a request body become code.
      - name: write the member file
        id: render
        env:
          MEMBER_JSON: ${{ inputs.member }}
          SOURCE_REPO: ${{ inputs.source_repository }}
          PROJECT_ID: ${{ inputs.project_id }}
          CHANGE_REF: ${{ inputs.change_ref }}
        run: |
          python3 - <<'PY'
          import datetime, json, os, pathlib, sys, yaml

          REVIEW_AFTER_DAYS = 180  # ta sama stala co w tools/render_member.py (kanal ticketowy)

          member = json.loads(os.environ["MEMBER_JSON"])
          source = os.environ["SOURCE_REPO"]

          # change_ref travels as its own input AND inside the declaration. They must agree: the flat one is
          # what the summary and the pull request quote, the nested one is what the OPA rules read.
          if member.get("change_ref") != os.environ["CHANGE_REF"]:
              sys.exit("change_ref w deklaracji rozni sie od change_ref w zgloszeniu")
          ref = member["change_ref"]

          # The concurrency group is built from the flat project_id, before any of this runs. If it named a
          # different project than the declaration, two requests for one project would run in parallel while
          # appearing serialised — so the disagreement is refused rather than resolved in favour of either.
          if member.get("project_id") != os.environ["PROJECT_ID"]:
              sys.exit(f"project_id w deklaracji ({member.get('project_id')!r}) rozni sie od "
                       f"project_id w zgloszeniu ({os.environ['PROJECT_ID']!r})")

          # change_ref MUST point at the repository that actually sent this. Otherwise a repository could
          # claim a reference belonging to someone else's PR and inherit their allowed-project list.
          if not ref.startswith(f"pr:{source}#"):
              sys.exit(f"change_ref {ref!r} nie wskazuje na repozytorium zgłaszające ({source})")

          out = pathlib.Path(f"perimeter/members/{member['division']}-{member['project_id']}.yaml")

          # An existing member file means this project is ALREADY in the perimeter. Overwriting it would
          # look like onboarding while rewriting someone's entry — including a silent demotion from
          # `enforced` back to `dry-run`, because the line below forces dry-run unconditionally. The project
          # would lose real protection through a PR titled "onboard (external)" that breaks no gate (a new
          # `dry-run` stage violates no promotion rule) and qualifies for auto-merge as an additive change.
          # The OPA duplicate rule cannot catch this one: it compares two member FILES, and re-requesting
          # the same division+project reuses the same filename, so a second file never appears.
          if out.exists():
              current = yaml.safe_load(out.read_text()) or {}
              sys.exit(
                  f"{out} juz istnieje — projekt {member['project_id']} jest czlonkiem perimetru "
                  f"(stage: {current.get('stage', '?')}); zmiane profili albo promocje zglos PR-em "
                  "na istniejacym pliku, nie kanalem wejsciowym")

          # Fields the REQUESTER does not own are written here, exactly like in the ServiceNow path
          # (tools/render_member.py). `stage` always starts at dry-run, no matter what the sender put in
          # the file — otherwise a single field in a payload would skip the whole two-stage onboarding.
          #
          # The dates are overwritten for a sharper reason than tidiness: `dry_run_since` is what the
          # promotion gate measures the observation window from. A backdated value ("2020-01-01") makes
          # that window look long past, so the 14 days of measurement DEC-4 exists for simply vanish.
          # A field describing WHEN the measurement started cannot come from the party being measured.
          dzis = datetime.date.today()
          member["stage"] = "dry-run"
          member["dry_run_since"] = dzis.isoformat()
          member["review_by"] = (dzis + datetime.timedelta(days=REVIEW_AFTER_DAYS)).isoformat()
          member.setdefault("exceptions", [])

          out.write_text(yaml.safe_dump(member, sort_keys=False, allow_unicode=True))
          print(f"zapisano {out}")

          # Branch name and PR title are built from these outputs rather than from the inputs context, so
          # no part of a request body is ever evaluated as an expression or as shell. (Not even in a
          # comment: actionlint parses expression syntax inside `run:`, heredoc or not.)
          with open(os.environ["GITHUB_OUTPUT"], "a") as fh:
              fh.write(f"division={member['division']}\nproject_id={member['project_id']}\n")
          PY

      - name: schema
        run: check-jsonschema --schemafile schemas/member.schema.json perimeter/members/*.yaml

      # This is where contributors.yaml does its work: is this repository allowed to ask for THIS project,
      # and does its division match the entry?
      - name: ownership and onboarding rules
        run: |
          python3 tools/collect_declarations.py > declarations.json
          conftest test --policy policy --namespace vpcsc.onboarding declarations.json

      - name: open the pull request
        uses: peter-evans/create-pull-request@c5a7806660adbe173f04e3e038b0ccdcd758773c # v6.1.0
        with:
          branch: external/${{ steps.render.outputs.division }}-${{ steps.render.outputs.project_id }}
          title: "onboard (external): ${{ steps.render.outputs.division }} / ${{ steps.render.outputs.project_id }}"
          commit-message: "onboard: ${{ steps.render.outputs.project_id }} requested by ${{ inputs.source_repository }}"
          body: |
            Requested from **${{ inputs.source_repository }}**
            (commit `${{ inputs.source_sha }}`), dispatched by `${{ github.triggering_actor }}`.

            Verified before this PR was opened:
            - the request was handled by the default branch, not by a leftover feature branch,
            - `change_ref` points at the dispatching repository,
            - that repository is allowed to request this project (`perimeter/contributors.yaml`),
            - the declared division matches the one mapped to that repository,
            - the member enters the **dry-run** configuration — nothing is blocked, nothing is protected yet.

            `triggering_actor` names the App installation that sent this, not the repository — one App on
            many division repositories yields one actor. The repository claim is bounded by
            `contributors.yaml`, and by nothing else.

            Still to be checked by a reviewer: whether the requested profiles match the described use case,
            and whether the project's network prerequisites are in place (pre-flight runs on `plan`).
          labels: onboarding, dry-run, external
